> If that's a buggy heuristic, then it's one that you introduced, @hartkopp, and reverting this MR wouldn't change that. So I don't understand your comment.

Ehm, no. You probably got that code wrong.

```
            /* CAN CC/FD */
            if ((tvb_reported_length(tvb) == 72) || (canfd_flags & CANFD_FDF)) {
                /* CAN FD: check for min/max data length */
                if ((tvb_reported_length(tvb) >= 8) && (tvb_reported_length(tvb) <= 72))
                    can_packet_type = PACKET_TYPE_CAN_FD;
            } else if ((tvb_reported_length(tvb) >= 8) && (tvb_reported_length(tvb) <= 16))
                can_packet_type = PACKET_TYPE_CAN;

```

The check for CAN FD is either the tvb_reported_length(tvb) == 72 (length detection) or with the CANFD_FDF flag.

When we entered this section (e.g. via CANFD_FDF condition) the possible data lengths from 0 to 64 are ensured. And if this is not the case, this was not a valid CAN FD frame that should be dropped. This check allows "unpadded" CAN FD frames with the CANFD_FDF flag set.

I never make a distinction whether a frame is CAN FD or CAN CC only based on the CAN data length - because this is not a valid identification for CC/FD.

@giovanni.musto Regarding my question "do you have a Wireshark version, that replays your file correctly?"

Can you name such a version?

If not, you might probably patch the pcapng file and set the FDF bit for the CAN frames you are sure to be CAN FD frames.

@giovanni.musto to summarize it up I would suggest the CAN frame type detection to look like the code below.

The changes in detail:
1. Restore the original detection rules
2. Remove some parenthesis as suggested
3. Drop invalid (undecodable) frames as discussed

For the last point I would like to pick up your suggestion to provide some expert information so that invalid frames can be detected and analyzed. Displaying the first 8 octets and the tvb_captured_length() should give the relevant information for the failure analysis. I don't know how this "expert info" can be generated, so that it is a win for the user. Maybe you can contribute some code for that?

```
        canfd_flags = tvb_get_uint8(tvb, CANFD_FLAG_OFFSET);
        canxl_flags = tvb_get_uint8(tvb, CANXL_FLAGS_OFFSET);

        if (canxl_flags & CANXL_XLF) {
            /* CAN XL: check for min/max data length */
            if (tvb_reported_length(tvb) >= 13 && tvb_reported_length(tvb) <= 2060) {
                can_packet_type = PACKET_TYPE_CAN_XL;
            } else {
                /* invalid CAN XL frame (expert info) */
                return tvb_captured_length(tvb);
            }
        } else {
            /* CAN CC/FD */
            if (tvb_reported_length(tvb) == 72 || (canfd_flags & CANFD_FDF)) {
                /* CAN FD: check for min/max data length */
                if (tvb_reported_length(tvb) >= 8 && tvb_reported_length(tvb) <= 72) {
                    can_packet_type = PACKET_TYPE_CAN_FD;
                } else {
                    /* invalid CAN FD frame (expert info) */
                    return tvb_captured_length(tvb);
                }
            } else if (tvb_reported_length(tvb) >= 8 && tvb_reported_length(tvb) <= 16) {
                can_packet_type = PACKET_TYPE_CAN;
            } else {
                /* invalid CAN CC frame (expert info) */
                return tvb_captured_length(tvb);
            }
        }
```

